INT. MATTRESS SHOWROOM, BACK OFFICE - DAY

A windowless room behind rows of beds. A desk, three chairs, a poster of a smiling sheep. The air smells of fresh foam and old coffee.

MORTON GRIGGS, fifties, in a short-sleeved shirt and a tie that has lost an argument with a stapler, sits behind the desk. He is the store manager. Facing him, in matching chairs, are DELPHINE ABERNATHY, thirties, an assistant manager with a binder, and WALLACE "WALLY" TSUJI, twenties, a salesman with a neck brace.

Morton folds his hands. He looks at both of them, and he sighs.

MORTON
We are here to resolve a grievance.

DELPHINE
I filed the grievance.

WALLY
I filed the counter-grievance.

MORTON
There's a form for that?

DELPHINE
There is now.

Wally shifts. His neck brace creaks. He glares at Delphine, then at the sheep poster, as though looking for an ally.

WALLY
Is it fair to say that a man has a right to nap on a display bed?

MORTON
No.

WALLY
Even during a lull?

MORTON
Especially during a lull.

DELPHINE
He was snoring, Morton. Customers thought it was the Sleep Number demo.

WALLY
The Sleep Number demo was broken.

DELPHINE
Because you broke it.

WALLY
I fell asleep on it. That's different.

MORTON
How is that different?

WALLY
I wasn't being malicious.

DELPHINE
You were being unconscious.

MORTON
Okay. Okay. Let me read the incident report.

Morton picks up a sheet of paper. Delphine slides her binder forward an inch. Wally leans back and the neck brace creaks again.

MORTON (CONT'D)
"At 2:14 PM, Mr. Tsuji was observed in Display Bed Nine, horizontal, with a pillow over his face."

WALLY
It was a decorative pillow.

MORTON
"Customers were advised that the model was a sample of our new Eternal Rest line."

DELPHINE
Several customers wished to purchase it.

MORTON
Wait. They did?

DELPHINE
Four units. A man from the funeral home on Fifth asked about wholesale.

MORTON
Well.

WALLY
That's a win.

DELPHINE
It is not a win. It's a liability.

MORTON
It's a win and a liability. Both can be true.

WALLY
That's what I've been saying.

DELPHINE
You have not been saying that. You have been saying "zzz."

MORTON
Let's focus. Delphine, what do you want?

DELPHINE
A written apology. And I want him to stop sleeping in the store.

MORTON
Wally?

WALLY
I want my sick days restored. And a new neck brace. This one pinches.

MORTON
Why do you need a neck brace?

WALLY
I fell off the bed.

DELPHINE
He rolled off Bed Nine during a nap and tried to file a worker's comp claim.

MORTON
It's a claim, Delphine. We have to hear it.

DELPHINE
Hear it? He was asleep.

WALLY
It was a work-related sleep. I was dreaming about quotas.

MORTON
Wally, that's not a thing.

WALLY
It is if you're committed.

Morton rubs his temples. He picks up a pen and writes something. He tears the page from the pad and hands it to Delphine, then writes another and hands it to Wally.

DELPHINE
What's this?

MORTON
Your apology.

WALLY
What's this?

MORTON
Yours.

DELPHINE
I don't owe him an apology.

MORTON
You gave him a Sleep Number quote he couldn't afford.

DELPHINE
That was a clerical error.

MORTON
So is this. Sign.

They sign. Morton collects the papers. He stands, and the chair rolls back into the filing cabinet with a bang. The sheep on the poster goes on smiling.

MORTON (CONT'D)
Meeting adjourned. Everyone back to the floor.

WALLY
Can I sit down on a bed first?

MORTON
No.
